(2603077, 354635, 38496, '<td valign="top">Hi Rebecca,<br/>Another shot of your toenails Ü.  This is cute.  Well, done technically.  The only complaint is that it''s not overly exciting.  It''s nice, but not ''wow'', and there isn''t quite enough emphasis on the penny.</td>', 'Hi Rebecca,Another shot of your toenails Ü.  This is cute.  Well, done technically.  The only complaint is that it''s not overly exciting.  It''s nice, but not ''wow'', and there isn''t quite enough emphasis on the penny.', '2006-07-09 12:54:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2603273, 353419, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Okay, first I had to say I had NO IDEA what you were talking about.  The first thing that came to mind was hair loss because of the type of hair in the hand and the way it''s arranged.<br/> <br/>Then I read some of the comments and thought, "I never heard it that way!"  :)<br/> <br/>So, now that I know what you are going for...........<br/> <br/>The lighting and tone set in this shot is very well done and expresses the setting that you were going for very nicely.  But honestly the shoulder in the lower left corner?  Very distracting and at first glance I couldn''t tell what it was, shoulder, breast......?  But bringing the shoulder back so it''s not taking up so much of the shot might help quite a bit.  I realize we risk showing something else when we do that but if you change the angle of the shot just a touch you could still get the message across while improving the overall composition of the shot.  I tried just cropping it out but that leaves the shot very unbalanced.<br/> <br/>Also the direction of the hand, it the fingers were pointing more towards the corner, offering a line through the shot it, again might help with the overall feel of the shot.<br/> <br/>Now, the "hair".  As mentioned previously, it just doesn''t look realistic.  You could have gone with some glue and some short hair or even string set up to make it look more like your leg or arm hair and this would have really sent the message across without any question.<br/> <br/>Again, very nice tones and the setting is clear as a bell.  I hope my comments help and Good Luck in Future Challenges.<br/> <br/>Deannda</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Okay, first I had to say I had NO IDEA what you were talking about.  The first thing that came to mind was hair loss because of the type of hair in the hand and the way it''s arranged. Then I read some of the comments and thought, "I never heard it that way!"  :) So, now that I know what you are going for........... The lighting and tone set in this shot is very well done and expresses the setting that you were going for very nicely.  But honestly the shoulder in the lower left corner?  Very distracting and at first glance I couldn''t tell what it was, shoulder, breast......?  But bringing the shoulder back so it''s not taking up so much of the shot might help quite a bit.  I realize we risk showing something else when we do that but if you change the angle of the shot just a touch you could still get the message across while improving the overall composition of the shot.  I tried just cropping it out but that leaves the shot very unbalanced. Also the direction of the hand, it the fingers were pointing more towards the corner, offering a line through the shot it, again might help with the overall feel of the shot. Now, the "hair".  As mentioned previously, it just doesn''t look realistic.  You could have gone with some glue and some short hair or even string set up to make it look more like your leg or arm hair and this would have really sent the message across without any question. Again, very nice tones and the setting is clear as a bell.  I hope my comments help and Good Luck in Future Challenges. Deannda', '2006-07-09 14:46:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(2603408, 353442, 47933, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club Sarah!<br/> <br/>Congrats on your best score to date. You certainly deserved it with this fine submission. I think the idea here was very creative and the blue tone works nicely to give us an eerie feel. <br/> <br/>Only a couple of things I''d critique here. The open space at the top left hinders the flow of the shot. While it does show that it''s a gameboard, it leaves just a bit. The hands on the planchette are a wonderful touch. However, I''d like to see just a tad more detail - not a lot more mind you but just enough to see some texture. <br/> <br/>Overall I really like this shot. Very creative indeed and one you should be proud of.<br/> <br/>Thanks for the opportunity to critique this image Sarah.<br/> <br/>Cheers - Phil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Sarah! Congrats on your best score to date. You certainly deserved it with this fine submission. I think the idea here was very creative and the blue tone works nicely to give us an eerie feel.  Only a couple of things I''d critique here. The open space at the top left hinders the flow of the shot. While it does show that it''s a gameboard, it leaves just a bit. The hands on the planchette are a wonderful touch. However, I''d like to see just a tad more detail - not a lot more mind you but just enough to see some texture.  Overall I really like this shot. Very creative indeed and one you should be proud of. Thanks for the opportunity to critique this image Sarah. Cheers - Phil', '2006-07-09 16:37:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(2603930, 353419, 57260, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the critique. I appreciate it.</td>', 'Thanks for the critique. I appreciate it.', '2006-07-09 19:32:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(2605115, 354316, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>WOW!  What a shot!  What a beautiful, wonderful shot!  And yes, I do get the reference being the owner of several animals, small birds included and remember hearing about the Febreeze scare.  <br/> <br/>So you title makes me laugh and it fits his expression.   I just love big birds and this shot is amazing to me.<br/> <br/>Looking at your score I''m betting you got hit more with DNMC votes than votes on the shot itself.  There is little here I can offer in the way of critique.  It''s well composed, lighting is a bit harsh but not overly so, the colors are wonderful, maybe could be tweeked just a touch to make them pop a little more, a touch more levels or curves adjustment, just a touch and a touch of sharpening to really define the eyes and feathers.  Other than that I can''t see any other really technical things you can do with this shot.  The wing is leading me in to that beautiful face and eye and the feathers around his eye and on top of head help me back out the other side.  You might have loosened the crop just a bit on the right for a touch more balance and maybe crop a touch more off the bottom, maybe putting his eye on the top third for the rule of thirds.<br/> <br/>But honestly I think your score is more a DNMC reflection because not a lot of people have heard about the Febreeze legend unless they also had animals.<br/> <br/>I hope my comments help and Good Luck In Future Challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! WOW!  What a shot!  What a beautiful, wonderful shot!  And yes, I do get the reference being the owner of several animals, small birds included and remember hearing about the Febreeze scare.   So you title makes me laugh and it fits his expression.   I just love big birds and this shot is amazing to me. Looking at your score I''m betting you got hit more with DNMC votes than votes on the shot itself.  There is little here I can offer in the way of critique.  It''s well composed, lighting is a bit harsh but not overly so, the colors are wonderful, maybe could be tweeked just a touch to make them pop a little more, a touch more levels or curves adjustment, just a touch and a touch of sharpening to really define the eyes and feathers.  Other than that I can''t see any other really technical things you can do with this shot.  The wing is leading me in to that beautiful face and eye and the feathers around his eye and on top of head help me back out the other side.  You might have loosened the crop just a bit on the right for a touch more balance and maybe crop a touch more off the bottom, maybe putting his eye on the top third for the rule of thirds. But honestly I think your score is more a DNMC reflection because not a lot of people have heard about the Febreeze legend unless they also had animals. I hope my comments help and Good Luck In Future Challenges! Deannda', '2006-07-10 00:54:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2607823, 354733, 19959, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club..<br/> <br/>Hi Pierre-André<br/> <br/>I actually quite like this picture, it meets the challenge and unlike a number of entries does not require an explanation in the title. Nice work.<br/> <br/>There have been some useful (and some not so!) comments from voters. The main rub being that the photo seems to be OOF (out of focus) I think it is actually motion blur (looking at the 1/8 second exposure) and reading that this was taken in the light from headlights.<br/> <br/>If there is one thing (other than not meeting a challenge) that will kill a score it is OOF. Voters do not spend enough time on each picture to pick up subtle differences between focus and motion. Although Yontally picked up on this and has a good point... either much more movement or non at all.<br/> <br/>The foot is quite central and does not meet the usual rule of thirds composition rules but it doesn''t need to. The foot and crack are central to the theme and central in the composition. Perfect (IMO)<br/> <br/>I guess that this doesn''t quite have the WOW factor to differentiate it although Kudos for shooting ''on location and improvising light. You have done very well considering the challenges involved!<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work, your creativity and ideas are excellent and I confidently predict some high scores in your future. I looked at your portfolio and really liked your ''Hands'' entry which unfortunately got a DQ.<br/> <br/>Regards<br/> <br/>Chris<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club.. Hi Pierre-André I actually quite like this picture, it meets the challenge and unlike a number of entries does not require an explanation in the title. Nice work. There have been some useful (and some not so!) comments from voters. The main rub being that the photo seems to be OOF (out of focus) I think it is actually motion blur (looking at the 1/8 second exposure) and reading that this was taken in the light from headlights. If there is one thing (other than not meeting a challenge) that will kill a score it is OOF. Voters do not spend enough time on each picture to pick up subtle differences between focus and motion. Although Yontally picked up on this and has a good point... either much more movement or non at all. The foot is quite central and does not meet the usual rule of thirds composition rules but it doesn''t need to. The foot and crack are central to the theme and central in the composition. Perfect (IMO) I guess that this doesn''t quite have the WOW factor to differentiate it although Kudos for shooting ''on location and improvising light. You have done very well considering the challenges involved! Keep up the good work, your creativity and ideas are excellent and I confidently predict some high scores in your future. I looked at your portfolio and really liked your ''Hands'' entry which unfortunately got a DQ. Regards Chris ', '2006-07-10 16:51:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(2609251, 354557, 52713, '<td valign="top">---Greetings from the Critique Club!---<br/> <br/>First impression -  OUCH! :-)<br/> <br/>First off - this is a very strong image as evident by the 6+ score. Technically very good. Rather than go through the exposure, composition, technique issues, let me try to tell you what would have kicked this score up into the top 10 or higher.<br/> <br/>When I first saw this image I didn''t get the heart thing. I don''t always read the title although I should. My feeling is if you have to rely on the title too much you''re probably not going to score very high.<br/> The heart thing along with the voodoo is an excellent idea. Titling the voodoo doll up a bit would accomplish 2 things:<br/> <br/>1. The viewers could make out the heart shape of the pins better,<br/>2. The DOF would not be as shallow. <br/> <br/>I like the shallow DOF, but I think it is distracting from the image here instead of enhancing it. Positioning the camera a little higher and s little more on top would have the same effect as tilting the doll up a bit.<br/> <br/>Again, I think this is one of the stronger images in the challenge. These are just my ideas to improve the placement. Congrats on a top 20 placement!<br/> <br/>I hope this was helpful. Feel free to contact me if you have any questions.<br/> <br/>David<br/></td>', '---Greetings from the Critique Club!--- First impression -  OUCH! :-) First off - this is a very strong image as evident by the 6+ score. Technically very good. Rather than go through the exposure, composition, technique issues, let me try to tell you what would have kicked this score up into the top 10 or higher. When I first saw this image I didn''t get the heart thing. I don''t always read the title although I should. My feeling is if you have to rely on the title too much you''re probably not going to score very high. The heart thing along with the voodoo is an excellent idea. Titling the voodoo doll up a bit would accomplish 2 things: 1. The viewers could make out the heart shape of the pins better,2. The DOF would not be as shallow.  I like the shallow DOF, but I think it is distracting from the image here instead of enhancing it. Positioning the camera a little higher and s little more on top would have the same effect as tilting the doll up a bit. Again, I think this is one of the stronger images in the challenge. These are just my ideas to improve the placement. Congrats on a top 20 placement! I hope this was helpful. Feel free to contact me if you have any questions. David', '2006-07-10 23:59:10.000000', NULL, 0),
